::ENG2_U04_L02_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Research question: How could later bus service affect after-school job access for high school students? Source A: A 2025 city transportation report lists evening routes, ridership numbers, and survey responses from high school students who work after school. Which evaluation is strongest?{
=A. Source A is useful because it is current, relevant, and includes data connected to the research question.#Correct. The source fits the question and provides evidence.
~B. Source A is weak because all reports are fiction.#Incorrect. Nothing in the source card supports that claim.
~C. Source A is biased because it mentions students.#Incorrect. Mentioning students is relevant, not automatically biased.
~D. Source A is useless because it has numbers.#Incorrect. Numbers can provide useful evidence.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Source B says, "Buses are always terrible and nobody should rely on them." Which phrase most clearly shows loaded language?{
~A. "Buses"#Incorrect. This is the topic, not loaded language.
=B. "always terrible"#Correct. This emotional phrase pushes a negative reaction without evidence.
~C. "rely on them"#Incorrect. This phrase is not the strongest loaded wording.
~D. "and"#Incorrect. This is a connecting word.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Source C is a bus company advertisement about comfortable seats. The research question is about job access for students. What is the main limitation?{
~A. It is too old because no date is given in the question.#Incorrect. The source card's main problem is relevance to job access.
~B. It is a historical speech.#Incorrect. It is described as an advertisement.
=C. It does not directly address student jobs or evening access.#Correct. It may describe buses, but it does not answer the research question.
~D. It gives too much academic evidence.#Incorrect. The source card does not describe evidence about job access.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Research question: How could compost bins reduce cafeteria trash? Which source is most valid?{
~A. A student's comment saying compost is "gross."#Incorrect. This is opinion and does not answer the research question.
~B. A cafeteria poster with only a picture of an apple.#Incorrect. This does not provide evidence about trash reduction.
~C. A product ad that says one bin is "perfect for everyone."#Incorrect. This is promotional and broad.
=D. A sanitation report comparing weekly trash weights before and after compost bins were added.#Correct. This evidence directly fits the research question.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] A source is credible when:{
=A. the author or organization has relevant knowledge or authority.#Correct. Credibility depends partly on expertise and authority.
~B. it uses the most emotional words.#Incorrect. Emotional words do not prove credibility.
~C. it has no connection to the topic.#Incorrect. Relevance matters.
~D. it avoids all evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence helps support credibility.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Research question: How could a school garden support science learning? Which source is least useful?{
~A. A university extension article about garden-based science observations.#Incorrect. This is highly relevant.
=B. A garden supply advertisement that only lists tool prices.#Correct. Tool prices do not answer the science-learning question.
~C. A teacher guide explaining plant-growth observations.#Incorrect. This connects directly to science learning.
~D. A district report about outdoor classroom use.#Incorrect. This may provide relevant context.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which question best tests source validity?{
~A. Does the source use a large font?#Incorrect. Font size does not prove validity.
~B. Does the source have a colorful image?#Incorrect. Images do not prove the evidence fits the question.
=C. Does the evidence in the source actually support the claim or research question?#Correct. Validity is about whether evidence fits and supports the inquiry.
~D. Does the title sound exciting?#Incorrect. Excitement does not prove validity.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] A source was published twenty years ago, but the research question is about current bus routes. What should the researcher notice?{
~A. The source is automatically best because it is old.#Incorrect. Older sources are not automatically better.
=B. The source may be outdated for a current-route question.#Correct. Current route research needs recent information.
~C. The source must be biased because all old sources are biased.#Incorrect. Age alone does not prove bias.
~D. The source is valid because dates never matter.#Incorrect. Dates can matter a lot for current topics.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Research question: How could a pre-order lunch form reduce cafeteria food waste? Which source is most relevant?{
=A. A school nutrition report measuring tray waste before and after a pre-order pilot.#Correct. It directly addresses the solution and effect in the question.
~B. A menu listing this week's pizza choices.#Incorrect. It does not discuss waste reduction.
~C. A social media comment saying forms are annoying.#Incorrect. This is opinion without evidence.
~D. An advertisement for cafeteria trays.#Incorrect. It does not answer the pre-order question.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which sentence is most neutral and least biased?{
~A. Only foolish schools avoid pre-order forms.#Incorrect. This attacks one side.
=B. Pre-order forms may reduce waste if students choose meals before lunch is prepared.#Correct. This sentence is cautious and evidence-based.
~C. Anyone who dislikes forms is wrong.#Incorrect. This is biased and dismissive.
~D. Forms are always the greatest solution ever.#Incorrect. This is exaggerated.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which source detail most improves credibility?{
~A. The author is unnamed and gives no evidence.#Incorrect. This weakens credibility.
~B. The source uses insults.#Incorrect. Insults do not improve credibility.
=C. The author is a science education specialist writing for a state university extension.#Correct. The author's role and organization support credibility.
~D. The source says it is always right.#Incorrect. A claim of being right is not evidence.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Two sources discuss bus service. Source A uses ridership data and student surveys. Source B says buses are "always terrible" with no evidence. Which comparison is strongest?{
~A. Source B is stronger because it sounds angry.#Incorrect. Anger is not evidence.
~B. Both sources are equally valid because both mention buses.#Incorrect. Mentioning the same topic does not make evidence equal.
~C. Source A should be rejected because it has data.#Incorrect. Data can strengthen a source.
=D. Source A is more valid because it supports its claim with relevant evidence.#Correct. Validity depends on evidence that supports the claim.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which source purpose should make a researcher read carefully for possible bias?{
=A. To sell a product related to the topic.#Correct. Promotional purpose may shape what information is emphasized or left out.
~B. To publish measured results from a school pilot.#Incorrect. This can still be checked, but it is not automatically promotional.
~C. To define a vocabulary term in a dictionary.#Incorrect. A dictionary definition may be limited, but its purpose is not sales.
~D. To report data collection methods clearly.#Incorrect. Clear methods support reliability.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Research question: What maintenance plan would help a school garden remain usable during summer break? Which source best fits?{
~A. A menu from the cafeteria.#Incorrect. It does not address garden maintenance.
=B. A garden club guide explaining weekly watering schedules and volunteer roles.#Correct. This directly addresses maintenance planning.
~C. A comment saying gardens are pretty.#Incorrect. This is opinion and not maintenance evidence.
~D. An ad for decorative pots only.#Incorrect. Product decoration does not answer the maintenance question.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Why is relevance important when choosing a source?{
~A. A source can only be used if it has no title.#Incorrect. Titles are not the issue.
~B. Relevance means the source is emotional.#Incorrect. Relevance is about fit.
=C. A source must help answer the actual research question.#Correct. Reliable research uses sources that fit the inquiry.
~D. Relevance means all sources are equal.#Incorrect. Sources can differ in usefulness and reliability.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] A source gives exact ridership numbers but does not explain where the numbers came from. What should a researcher do?{
~A. Accept it without question because all numbers are reliable.#Incorrect. Numbers need context and source information.
~B. Reject all data forever.#Incorrect. Data can be useful when methods are clear.
~C. Use it as the only source.#Incorrect. A researcher should verify unclear evidence.
=D. Look for information about the data source or method before relying on it.#Correct. Research requires checking how evidence was collected.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which word from a source most suggests bias?{
=A. "foolish"#Correct. This word attacks a group and pushes an emotional judgment.
~B. "updated"#Incorrect. This word describes currency.
~C. "report"#Incorrect. This word names a source type.
~D. "survey"#Incorrect. This word names an evidence method.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which source is reliable for current school policy research?{
~A. An anonymous post with no evidence.#Incorrect. This lacks authority and evidence.
=B. A recent district report that explains its data and methods.#Correct. It is current, relevant, and transparent about evidence.
~C. A twenty-year-old rumor.#Incorrect. This is outdated and unsupported.
~D. An advertisement with no research data.#Incorrect. Promotional sources need careful checking and may not provide evidence.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Source summary: A report says evening library hours help students who cannot visit before 5:00 p.m. What is the central idea?{
~A. Libraries should remove all books.#Incorrect. This is not stated.
=B. Evening hours may increase access for students with daytime conflicts.#Correct. This captures the main point of the summary.
~C. Students never use libraries.#Incorrect. The summary says later hours could help students.
~D. Time does not matter for library access.#Incorrect. Time is central to the idea.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which source would be weakest for a research question about student job access and buses?{
~A. A city transportation report with student survey data.#Incorrect. This is relevant and evidence-based.
~B. An interview with students who use buses after school.#Incorrect. This may provide relevant evidence.
~C. Current evening route schedules.#Incorrect. Route schedules are relevant.
=D. A recipe website with no information about buses or jobs.#Correct. It is unrelated to the research question.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which explanation best justifies choosing a source?{
=A. I chose the district report because it directly addresses the question and includes measured data.#Correct. This explanation connects source choice to relevance and evidence.
~B. I chose it because it was first in the list.#Incorrect. Order in a list does not prove reliability.
~C. I chose it because it sounded dramatic.#Incorrect. Drama is not evidence.
~D. I chose it because it agreed with me.#Incorrect. Confirmation is not the same as reliability.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] What is the best reason to avoid using only advertisements as research sources?{
~A. Advertisements always contain no words.#Incorrect. Ads can contain words.
=B. Advertisements are designed to sell, so they may emphasize benefits and omit limitations.#Correct. Promotional purpose can create bias.
~C. Advertisements are always written by historians.#Incorrect. This is not true.
~D. Advertisements cannot mention products.#Incorrect. Ads usually do mention products.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which pair of source features is strongest?{
~A. Anonymous author and no evidence.#Incorrect. This weakens reliability.
~B. Loaded language and no data.#Incorrect. This suggests bias.
=C. Relevant expert author and clearly explained evidence.#Correct. These features support credibility and validity.
~D. Sales purpose and unrelated topic.#Incorrect. These features weaken usefulness.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Research question: How could pre-order forms reduce waste? Source: A student says, "I hate forms." What is the best evaluation?{
~A. The source is enough because it has a strong feeling.#Incorrect. Strong feeling is not enough for research.
~B. The source proves pre-order forms reduce waste.#Incorrect. It does not discuss waste reduction.
~C. The source is the most credible possible evidence.#Incorrect. It is a personal opinion.
=D. The source may show one opinion but is not enough to answer the research question.#Correct. It lacks evidence about waste reduction.
}

::ENG2_U04_L02_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which final checklist should a researcher use before selecting a source?{
=A. Does it answer my question, come from a credible author or organization, use evidence, and avoid major bias?#Correct. This checklist covers fit, credibility, evidence, and fairness.
~B. Does it have the biggest opinion and the fewest facts?#Incorrect. That would weaken research.
~C. Does it avoid the research question completely?#Incorrect. A source must fit the question.
~D. Does it use insults to sound confident?#Incorrect. Insults suggest bias, not reliability.
}
